From 80c6b09a919e304b9dae8520d266ca716a8021bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kbench <kbench@localhost>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 09:59:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] net: gro: look up the offload before walking the gro list

dev_gro_receive() calls gro_list_prepare() and only then searches for a
packet_offload with a ->gro_receive. For a protocol that has none --
ARP, and every other ethertype that cannot coalesce -- the walk is
thrown away at the 'normal' label, having already compared the packet
against up to MAX_GRO_SKBS held skbs, each comparison covering the
ethernet header, the vlan tag and the metadata mark.

Swap the two. gro_list_prepare() writes ->same_flow only on skbs that
are already queued, never on the incoming one, and nothing reads that
field before the next packet's gro_list_prepare() overwrites it, so a
packet that leaves through 'normal' loses nothing by not having run it.
Nothing between the two points modifies skb, and the NAPI_GRO_CB zeroing
still happens after gro_list_prepare() exactly as before.

On a router this matters more than the ARP share suggests: the same walk
is wasted whenever skb->hash is zero, because the bucket index and the
per-entry fast reject are both skb_get_hash_raw(), so every packet lands
in bucket 0 and no comparison can be skipped.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XVxrZjHzxnmyA7MDozho8A
---
 net/core/gro.c | 14 ++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/net/core/gro.c b/net/core/gro.c
index 29b4d02bf519..25e9ce796820 100644
--- a/net/core/gro.c
+++ b/net/core/gro.c
@@ -488,8 +488,16 @@ static enum gro_result dev_gro_receive(struct gro_node *gro,
 	if (netif_elide_gro(skb->dev))
 		goto normal;
 
-	gro_list_prepare(&gro_list->list, skb);
-
+	/* Find the offload before walking the gro list, not after.
+	 *
+	 * For a protocol with no ->gro_receive -- ARP, and every other
+	 * ethertype that cannot coalesce -- the walk below is thrown away at
+	 * the 'normal' label, after comparing the packet against up to
+	 * MAX_GRO_SKBS held skbs. gro_list_prepare() only writes ->same_flow
+	 * on skbs that are already queued, and nothing reads that before the
+	 * next packet's gro_list_prepare() overwrites it, so skipping it on
+	 * the way out changes nothing.
+	 */
 	rcu_read_lock();
 	list_for_each_entry_rcu(ptype, head, list) {
 		if (ptype->type == type && ptype->callbacks.gro_receive)
@@ -499,6 +507,8 @@ static enum gro_result dev_gro_receive(struct gro_node *gro,
 	goto normal;
 
 found_ptype:
+	gro_list_prepare(&gro_list->list, skb);
+
 	skb_set_network_header(skb, skb_gro_offset(skb));
 	skb_reset_mac_len(skb);
 	BUILD_BUG_ON(sizeof_field(struct napi_gro_cb, zeroed) != sizeof(u32));
